# Line endings, pinned per path.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so without these rules a checkout rewrites every text
# file to CRLF - including the JSON files this repo requires to be LF. That is not cosmetic: two
# tests compare RAW BYTES, and the failure they produce blames content when the cause is the
# checkout. It has bitten twice, most recently when a plain `git checkout Data/Custom/navigation.json`
# brought the file back as CRLF and the golden byte-identity test started failing on a file whose
# records were identical.
#
# See CLAUDE.md, "Line endings are not uniform in this tree".

# --- JSON that must be LF -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All of it is written by a program rather than typed: the shard through `AtomicFile.Write`, the
# editor through `compact.js`, and the reference through `tools/nav-import/uo-offline.js`. All
# three emit LF, so LF is what the file is, and `project.test.js` asserts the shipped files are
# byte-for-byte what the writer produces.

Data/Custom/navigation.json          text eol=lf
Data/Custom/britain-daily-life.json  text eol=lf
Data/Custom/restricted-zones.json    text eol=lf
Data/Custom/core-smoke.json          text eol=lf
Data/Custom/golden/*.json            text eol=lf
Data/Custom/reference/*.json         text eol=lf

# `bots.json` is deliberately absent: it is hand-edited, is CRLF today, and nothing compares its
# bytes. Pinning it would renormalise a file for no reason.

# --- C# stays CRLF --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Which is what it already checks out as. Stated rather than inherited from autocrlf, so a
# checkout on a machine configured differently produces the same tree.
#
# SCOPED TO OUR OWN CODE, deliberately. A bare `*.cs` reaches the whole of upstream ServUO, whose
# blobs were committed containing CRLF - 272 files, every line of each, would be rewritten to
# satisfy the rule. That is a line-ending-only diff across somebody else's tree, and it would make
# those files show as permanently modified until somebody took it. Our own files are the ones we
# write, and the ones a flip would actually cost us.

Scripts/Custom/**/*.cs text eol=crlf
